otal 12 tiles. I need two more melds and a pair. \n\nLooking at my hand: W2 (2), W3, T8, B2, B3, B4 (2). So possible combinations. Let's see. \n\nOption 1: If I can form a Pung of W2. I have two W2s. If I draw another W2, that would be a Pung. But there are 4 W2s total, and unshown shows W2:4. Wait, the unshown tiles list says W2:4. Wait, but in my hand, I have two W2s. So there are 4 - 2 = 2 left? Wait, the unshown tiles are the remaining counts. So if the unshown shows W2:4, that means there are 4 W2s left in the wall. Wait, no, the unshown tiles are the counts of each tile remaining. So the user provided \"Unshown tiles are: [W1: 2; W2: 4; ...]\" which means there are 2 W1s left, 4 W2s, etc. So for W2, there are 4 left. So if I have two in my hand, there are 4 in the wall. So the chance of drawing another W2 is possible.\n\nBut if I play a W2 now, someone might be looking for it. For example, the next player has a meld [B3, B4, B5], so maybe they are looking for B5. But if I play B4, maybe someone is looking for it. Let me check the melds of others. The previous player has [B4, B5, B6], so if I discard B4, they might have a B5 already and need a B4 to complete a Chow? Or they already have that meld. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, looking at my own hand. If I can form a Pung of B4. I have two B4s. If I draw another B4, that would be a Pung. There are 2 B4s left in the unshown. So possible. But the chance is lower than W2, which has 4 left.\n\nAnother possibility: if I can form a Chow in W2-W3-W4. I have W2, W3. If I get a W4, that's a Chow. There are 3 W4s left. So possible.\n\nAlternatively, the T8. If I have T8, maybe I can form a T7-T8-T9 Chow. But I don't have T7 or T9. The T8 is a single tile now.\n\nLooking at the melds of others. The previous player has a B8 Pung and a B4-B5-B6 Chow. The next player has a B1 Pung and a B3-B4-B5 Chow. The player across has some B9s and B4s. So the B4 is in multiple Chows. If I discard B4, someone might be looking for it. So maybe it's safer to discard a tile that's less likely to be needed by others.\n\nIn my hand, the tiles are W2, W2, W3, T8, B2, B3, B4, B4. The valid actions are Play W3; Play B3; Play W2; Play T8; Play B2; Play B4. So I need to choose one of these.\n\nIf I play W3, then I have W2, W2 left. Maybe someone is looking for a W3. The next player has a meld [B3, B4, B5], so maybe not. The previous player has a B4-B5-B6, so maybe not. The T8 is a single tile. If I play T8, maybe someone is looking for it? The T8 is in the T suit. The next player has a T2, and others have T2, T5, T9. Not sure.\n\nIf I play B2, then I have B3, B4, B4 left. Maybe someone is looking for B2? The next player has a B3 in their meld, so if they have B2, they could form a B2-B3-B4 Chow. But they already have B3 and B4 in their meld. Wait, the next player's meld is [B3, B4, B5], so they already have that. So if I discard B2, maybe someone else is looking for it? The previous player has a B4-B5-B6, so B2 is not part of that. The player across has a B2 in their history (they played B2). Maybe not.\n\nIf I play B3, then I have B2, B4, B4 left. The B3 is part of the B2-B3-B4 Chow. If I discard B3, someone might be looking for it. The previous player's meld has B4-B5-B6, so maybe not. The next player's meld has B3-B4-B5, so they already have B3. So maybe not.\n\nIf I play W2, then I have one W2 left. Maybe someone is looking for a W2? The unshown has 4 W2s left, so it's possible. But if I discard one, others might take it.\n\nIf I play B4, then I have B2, B3, B4 left. If someone is looking for a B4, they might take it. Since the next player has a B4 in their meld, and the previous player has a B4 in their meld, maybe they already have enough. But the B4 is in multiple Chows. So playing B4 might be risky.\n\nConsidering all that, maybe the safest tile to play is T8. Because it's a single tile in my hand, and there's no indication that others need it. Also, the T8 is in the T suit, which others have T2, T5, T9, etc., but no obvious Chow that needs T8. So playing T8 might be the safest. Alternatively, playing W3 could be risky if someone is looking for a W3 to complete a Chow. But since I have two W2s and a W3, maybe forming a W2-W3-W4 Chow is possible if I get a W4. So keeping W3 might be better for future possibilities.\n\nAlternatively, if I play B2, then I can have B3 and B4 left, which could form a B3-B4-B5 Chow if I get a B5. But there are B5s left (unshown B5:2). So maybe that's a possibility. However, the next player has a B3-B4-B5 meld, so if I discard B2, they might not need it. \n\nHmm. Another angle: the game history shows that the next player has played B3 and B4 in their Chi. The previous player has B4-B5-B6. So maybe the B4 is more common. If I play B4, there's a higher chance someone will take it. So to avoid giving others a chance to complete their hands, maybe it's better to play a tile that's less likely to be needed. \n\nBetween the options, T8 seems the least likely to be needed. So the answer is Play T8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The safest tile to discard is T8 as it is a single tile with no immediate risk of completing others' melds. Keeping W2 and B4 allows potential for future pungs or chows.\nReason: Discarding T8 avoids giving opponents tiles needed for their existing melds (B4, B5, W2). Retaining W2 and B4 supports possible future pung or chow formations.\nAnswer: Play T8"}
